## 상속

nheritance: a child class borrowing from a parent class

The key rule: to inherit, you put the parent class name inside the parentheses of the child class definition.

python
class VIPMember(Member):
#                 ↑ parent goes here in the parentheses

That single line is what connects them. It tells Python: "VIPMember is a kind of Member, so give it everything Member has."

What "everything Member has" means in your code:

python
class Member:
    def login(self):                      # ← parent defines login()
        print("로그인되었습니다.")


class VIPMember(Member):                  # ← inherits from Member
    def use_vip_lounge(self):             # ← child adds its own method
        print("VIP 라운지를 이용합니다.")

VIPMember only defines use_vip_lounge(). It never defines login(). But because it inherited from Member, it gets login() for free:

python
vip_user = VIPMember()

vip_user.login()           # ✅ borrowed from Member (parent)
vip_user.use_vip_lounge()  # ✅ its own method (child)

So vip_user can do both — the parent's methods and its own.

The mental model: think of it like "is-a." A VIP member is a member, so it can do anything a regular member can (log in), plus extra things only VIPs can (use the lounge).

Why this is useful: you don't have to rewrite login() inside VIPMember. If every kind of member needs to log in, you write login() once in Member, and every child class gets it automatically. Less repeated code, and if you ever fix login(), the fix applies everywhere at once.


